{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "source": "# Orchid service runbook\n\nThe backup job begins at 02:15. Its encrypted archive is copied to the western storage site. Nightly backups preserve a recoverable copy of the service data.\n\nA restore drill verifies that archived data can actually be recovered. Engineers restore a sample every second Tuesday. They compare the restored record count with the manifest.\n\nThe deployment dashboard has an amber status light. A canary release limits initial exposure to the new service version. Only five percent of traffic reaches the canary.\n\nThe old release remains available in the registry. If error rates exceed two percent for five minutes, traffic returns to the previous version. Automatic rollback contains failures during a canary deployment.\n\nProduction access requires a temporary credential approved by the duty engineer. The credential expires after forty minutes. Every approval is written to the access log.\n\nThe emergency account uses a sealed hardware token. Emergency access is reviewed after use even when the incident is resolved. The review must occur within one working day.\n",
  "source_name": "service.md",
